#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153233} :activity/id #uuid "49f2cd9a-dcc1-5758-b224-967071318eb9" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049702 :topic/id #uuid "0cbdba81-6911-563b-9935-b7f4c99e46ed" :topic/math-academy-id 2642} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153233 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153457 :tutorial/content "Recall that the residue class of $a$ modulo $n,$ denoted $[a]_n,$ is the set of integers that are congruent to $a$ modulo $n.$\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{ x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: | \\: x \\equiv a \\,  \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- The residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the even integers because all even integers have a remainder of $0$ when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{2}={0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the odd integers because all odd integers have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[1]_{2}={\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 5,\\,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the residue classes $[0]_2$ and $[1]_2$ *partition* the integers, meaning that any integer belongs either to $[0]_2$ or $[1]_2,$ but never both.\n\n![](images/89/8932542ec22de980b3b2e1a5a11a3edf2250efc7ea4f1ca64ca35aa4031c63c9.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "33bb31fa-3552-5d74-b6fc-efc1bc68b367" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9029 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a53d56de-6974-541c-ae3d-07f5110b4d87" :step/math-academy-id 452754 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153234}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153234 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153458 :tutorial/content "A residue class is a particular type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.**\n\nGiven an equivalence relation $\\sim$ on a set $A,$ the equivalence class of an element $a \\in A,$ denoted $[a],$ consists of all the elements of $A$ that are equivalent to $a.$\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{ x \\in A \\, | \\, x \\sim a \\}\n$$\n\nSince any equivalence relation is symmetric, we can also write\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{ x \\in A \\, | \\, a \\sim x \\}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if $|a|=|b|.$ \n\nUnder this relation, two integers are equivalent if they have the same absolute value. The first few equivalence classes are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|0|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=0} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|1|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=1} \\\\  & ={\\pm 1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|2|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=2} \\\\  & ={\\pm 2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|3|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=3} \\\\  & ={\\pm 3} \\\\  & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Equivalence relations can have a finite or infinite number of equivalence classes. This particular equivalence relation has an infinite number of equivalence classes.\n\n- For equivalence classes containing more than one element, we can use any element to represent the class. For example, $[1]$ and $[-1]$ represent the same equivalence class." :tutorial/id #uuid "67a56179-d17e-54ab-86a3-aeca86ce5a7d" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9030 :tutorial/title "Equivalence Classes"} :step/id #uuid "c058c41e-e32b-55dd-afbc-83f6fe827e97" :step/math-academy-id 452755 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153235}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153235 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151849 :question/id #uuid "b2dd8c4b-cabe-5a47-b6cd-b426f41280a5" :question/math-academy-id "e-14603"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3601ac83-0800-567a-9a0d-4d5c37cef413" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049833} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050597}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152864} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421718} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421722} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421723}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Elements Belonging to an Equivalence Class"} :step/id #uuid "9c1a0bc3-17ac-5f09-94bc-a0e1a78d66ce" :step/math-academy-id 452756 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153236}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153236 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151851 :question/id #uuid "bc24cde3-b907-5bc0-9889-a7a54b10a2a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-14605"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "dacfbca6-6135-5d51-a07e-627fce79dfd1" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050218}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152754} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421721} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421724} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421729}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Equivalence Classes Containing a Given Element"} :step/id #uuid "043a3d4c-6176-589b-b103-fa5c421defef" :step/math-academy-id 452760 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153237}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153237 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153480 :tutorial/content "The properties of residue classes generalize to equivalence classes. Let's list some of them:\n\n- If $a \\sim b$ then $[a]=[b].$ This allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n\n- On the other hand, two distinct equivalence classes have an empty intersection: \n\n$$\na \\not\\sim b\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n[a] \\cap [b] = \\emptyset\n$$\n\n- The residue classes form a partition of $A.$ Moreover, $A$ can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\nA = \\bigcup_{i\\in I}\\,[a]_i\n$$\n\nwhere $[a]_i$ denotes the $i$'th residue class and $I$ is an index set spanning all possible classes.\n\nConsider once again the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if $|a|=|b|.$ We found that the equivalence classes were as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={0} \\\\ [1] & ={\\pm 1} \\\\ [2] & ={\\pm 2} \\\\ [3] & ={\\pm 3} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese equivalence classes form a partition of the set of integers:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{[0], [1], [2],\\ldots\\big\\}\n$$\n\nMoreover, the integers can be constructed from the disjoint union of these classes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =[0]\\cup [1]\\cup [2]\\cup ⋯ \\\\  & ={0}\\cup {\\pm 1}\\cup {\\pm 2}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "6f51d5c2-6cda-51cd-9f6f-70d3f573d73b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 9358 :tutorial/title "Properties of Equivalence Classes"} :step/id #uuid "ef4eeaea-367c-522b-9b5b-d1817f4ffba0" :step/math-academy-id 452763 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153238}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186153238 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151852 :question/id #uuid "1e93cc7e-4adc-5a6a-94d1-966d67511a19" :question/math-academy-id "e-14606"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "21e3832f-b11f-5501-bf39-74665b88c38b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049842}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152831} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152834} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421720} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421725} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421726} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421727} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421728}] :knowledge-point/title "Counting Equivalence Classes"} :step/id #uuid "68ab214f-cc76-5969-a571-37e88e8c7f4c" :step/math-academy-id 452764}] :activity/title "Equivalence Classes" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186152337}]}
